ormation  hoping to use her reach to spread  information responsibly  and influence others to do their part to  end the pandemic  jamie morocco global news toronto a  search for unmarked graves got underway  today at the former delmos residential  school in saskatchewan  crews are using ground penetrating radar  to scour the former school grounds  the school was operated by the roman  catholic church from 1901  to 1948 when it burned down  coming up the call for political change  in communist cuba  in the communist country of cuba  speaking out can have dire consequences  so scenes like this are rare thousands  of cubans have taken to the streets to  show  they're not afraid and they're fed up  the pandemic  the poor economy a crisis over food and  medicine  and a lack of freedom have tempers  flaring reggie chikini tonight on the  descent born from desperation  and the hopes the world will take notice  the echoes of the largest revolt in  decades still resonate in the streets of  cuba  frustrated hungry and terrified  residents hurled their anger towards a  government  they see as having failed the country  this protester says the crowd's chanted  we want medicine  we want food  the island's coffers are empty there's a  critical food shortage the health care  system is in shambles  vaccine supply is limited  and after initially blaming america's  embargo for the crisis  cuba's president accepted some  responsibility a rare moment of  contrition  the government since it is somewhat  allergic  to doing real change maybe that's  what's happening that they're seeing  their survival at stake here  the protests driven by social media led  to an internet shutdown to prevent the  world from seeing the chaos  the uprising has fostered fears of a new  wave of defections by  boat but freedom a mere 120 kilometers  away  feels impossibly out of reach for most  if you take  to the sea you will not come  to the united states the biden  administration is facing mounting  pressure from the cuban exile community  to do something  i don't have anything to predict for you  in terms of any policy shift  the more aggressive in tone that the u.s  responds to this the greater the degree  to which  the government in cuba says see this is  an excuse for the u.s  to act aggressively ottawa has also been  criticized for taking a passive approach  to the crisis  but changed its tune on thursday we  stand as we always will  with the people of cuba who want and  deserve  democracy freedom and respect  the protest did lead to minor  concessions a temporary lift on  restrictions  limiting how much food or medicine  travelers can bring in  a band-aid solution to an economic  meltdown  part of what we're seeing is this call  for the government to be  much more accountable and to essentially  say you know deal with this or leave  you know and let somebody else do it  even if fractures formed from this  historic pushback the cuban government  is giving no indication it intends to  further bend  reggie kinney global news washington  still ahead the species at risk why are  manatees dying at an alarming rate  don't blink or you'll miss it it might  look like a bug on your screen but that  is the international space station seen  transiting  past the moon the site was captured from  spain's canary islands in slow motion  according to nasa the space station  travels at a speed of nearly 25 000  kilometers per hour  as it orbits earth approximately 16  times a day  the race is on tonight to stop an  ecological disaster in the waters of  florida  and save the state's marine mammal  manatees are known to be quite resilient  learning to adapt to the environment  around them  but this year an unprecedented number  has perished  jackson proska explains why these sea  creatures are starving to death  and who's to blame there's a reason  they're called sea cows  manatees are the gentle giants of the  ocean  in normal times they lumber along  searching for warm water  and grazing on sea grass but this year  has proven especially deadly and  difficult for the species  manatees are dying off at an alarming  rate  the man is actually a really good  indicator of what's happening in the  environment they're very key species  so seeing so many manatees die is  actually an indicator that there is  definitely something wrong  something very wrong with the ecosystem  at least 840 manatees have died  so far this year in florida far  surpassing the total number of deaths  for all of 2020  more often than not they're starving the  seagrass they eat is being choked out by  algae blooms  fertilizers and runoff end up in the  water feeding the blooms  which block the sunlight needed to grow  the manatees food supply  it's affecting all the fish the crabs  everything that's  dependent on the water out here and sea  grass florida's  saved the manatee club recently rescued  and rehabilitated an emaciated manatee  named mandy  along with her calf mandy weighed just  400 kilograms  she should have weighed over 1 000 that  just shows you  how amazing that she really was when she  was rescued and  that's the case with a lot of manatees  out there a devastating outbreak of red  tide in florida has only added to the  manatees woes  they're being killed off along with  thousands of fish and other sea mammals  the situation is now so dire  environmentalists are pushing for  manatees to be added back to the  endangered species list  it's a problem that we cannot fix  overnight but first and foremost we do  need to clean up the water quality we do  need to replant sea grasses  time is not on the manatees side they  reproduce slowly  and at current rates up to one third of  florida's manatee population could die  this year it's a man-made disaster  that threatens this beloved ocean mammal  jackson prosko global news washington  up next the canadian entrepreneur who is  giving back to indigenous communities  a canadian entrepreneur is making a name  for herself in the makeup industry  jennifer harper started cheekbone beauty  with only five hundred dollars  now she's taking orders from the other  side of the world but her company is  about more than just cosmetics as kayla  mclean explains  it's about empowering indigenous people  to believe in themselves  so you just scoop it and scrape it in  owning her own lab in cosmetic line  cheekbone beauty is the stuff jennifer  harper's dreams are made of  literally i had a dream about lip gloss  and super happy dative little girls back  in 2015  and that night i literally grabbed my  laptop and started writing out the  business plan  from no industry experience to ceo of a  canadian made beauty brand receiving  orders as far as australia  some days i sit there and i'm like what  who's this what's happening here  it feels surreal partly because of how  much she's overcome  alcoholism identity issues and the pain  of losing her brother to suicide  learning her grandmother was a  residential school survivor made  everything click if there is a trauma  that does tend to go on for generations  unless someone chooses  to correct it or heal it understanding  the lasting effects of colonial abuse on  indigenous people propelled the  anishinabe entrepreneur to break the  cycle  empowered by her brother's last words to  her he said our youth need hope and they  need help  harper evolved cheekbone beauty into a  makeup brand where indigenous people can  feel supported  celebrated and seen original collection  was called the warrior women every one  of these shades  was named after a really inspiring  strong indigenous woman  and for every makeup product sold a  portion of the proceeds go back to  organizations supporting indigenous  communities  for us as a business it's about  supporting indigenous youth and sort of  any idea of any education endeavor  that they choose to go on the indie  brand is not only giving back to people  but the planet too so when the  lipstick's all done  you literally would just take the cap  and this can go in your compost and it  is biodegradable  created with sustainable ingredients and  packaging her products are named after  the words for land in several first  nations languages  reflecting the people's deep connection  to nature and duty to future generations  she's paving the way for us indigenous  regalia maker deanne hubfield who's  mentored by harper sees cheekbone  beauty's work as not only healing  but motivating no other indigenous  women are are doing a business like  first so it's  at this time it's really great to see  that we know that we're not the solution  to all of these big huge problems that  happen around us  but if we can be like a little glimmer  of light in in sort of a dark space then  we feel really good about the work we're  doing  kayla mclean global news and that is  global national for this saturday night  i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is lake huron in the  town of blind river ontario we would  love to see your corner of the country  so please  keep sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

